;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; BOOK-LAYOUT.slat — the folder-book boot-record convention
;;
;; "Design a system for chaptered books to be read from folders,
;;  where a README.slatl points to the beginning of a book like a
;;  boot record. It will be the nav in MD. And tells the LLM what's
;;  in there and what it's for. Repeated somewhat in the other books
;;  but it's fine — different audiences." — Alfred, 2026-07-24.
;;
;; This spec defines HOW a directory becomes a book: what files are
;; required, what the boot record does, and how a reader (human, LLM,
;; or Rosetta) boots into it. It applies to every CHAPTERED book in
;; the tree — scheme/reference/, scheme-books/book-of-*, SRE-MANUAL/,
;; docs/ (the engineering & recovery shelf). It does NOT apply to
;; plain utility folders (scratch/, scripts/, tools/) that merely
;; carry a README for orientation.
;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
(dialect :applies ("motoi" "sakura") :note "Same layout for both dialects. The sister dialect mirrors Motoi.")

(spec
  :name    "book-layout"
  :title   "Folder-Book Boot-Record Convention"
  :version "1"
  :status  "canonical"
  :written "2026-07-24"
  :authority "Alfred 2026-07-24 (folder-book directive)"
  :training-eligible #t
  :confidentiality "public"

  :what-this-is
  "A chaptered book is a DIRECTORY, not a single file. The directory
   holds ordered chapter files plus one boot record. Reading the book
   means: open the folder, read the boot record, follow it into the
   first chapter, walk the chapters in order. This spec is the rule
   every book folder obeys so any reader can boot into any book the
   same way."

  ;; ─── The two required files ──────────────────────────────────
  :required-files
  ((:file "README.slatl"
    :role "boot record"
    :one-per-book #t
    :purpose
    "The boot record. First thing any reader opens. Three jobs:
     (1) BOOT — name the book's first chapter file so a reader knows
         where the book begins (the :boot-into field).
     (2) NAV — carry the ordered chapter list; this list IS the
         navigation and renders directly to a Markdown table of
         contents on GitHub / in Rosetta.
     (3) BRIEF — tell an LLM (and a human) what this book IS, WHEN
         you would read it, and WHAT is inside — in prose, up front,
         so a cold reader with no other context understands the book
         before reading a single chapter.")
   (:file "MANIFEST.slat"
    :role "machine index"
    :one-per-book #t
    :optional-when "a small book may fold the manifest INTO the boot record's :chapters list; large books keep it separate."
    :purpose
    "The machine-readable index: every chapter with its number,
     file, library/section, and verb/record counts. Tools read this
     first for structure. The boot record is for humans+LLMs; the
     manifest is for programs. They must agree — same chapter order,
     same files."))

  ;; ─── The boot record's required fields ───────────────────────
  :boot-record-fields
  ((:field ":book-title"   :required #t :what "human title of the book")
   (:field ":book-kind"    :required #t :what "reference-manual | learning-book | engineering-shelf | operations-manual | …")
   (:field ":boot-into"    :required #t :what "filename of the FIRST chapter — the boot sector. A reader starts here.")
   (:field ":what-this-is" :required #t :what "prose: what the book is. One paragraph.")
   (:field ":when-to-read" :required #t :what "prose: the conditions under which you reach for this book. One paragraph.")
   (:field ":whats-inside" :required #t :what "prose: what a reader will find. One paragraph.")
   (:field ":nav"          :required #t :what "the ordered chapter list — (:n <k> :title <str> :file <name> :purpose <str>). This is the MD nav.")
   (:field ":not-here"     :required #f :what "prose: what this book is deliberately NOT — points at the sibling book that holds it.")
   (:field ":for"          :required #f :what "list of audiences (human-reader, llm-training, llm-inference, returning-collaborator, rosetta-render-target).")
   (:field ":for-rosetta"  :required #f :what "render hints: treat this folder as a book, render README as landing page, :nav as the TOC."))

  ;; ─── Boot semantics (why "boot record") ──────────────────────
  :boot-semantics
  "Like a disk boot sector: the README.slatl is a fixed, known entry
   point that hands control to the first chapter. A reader never has
   to guess where a book starts or in what order to read it — the
   boot record says :boot-into <first-chapter> and lists the rest in
   :nav order. An LLM booting cold reads README.slatl, learns the
   book's purpose and shape, then loads chapters on demand. Nothing
   is hidden; the boot record is a map, not a gate."

  ;; ─── Renders as Markdown nav ─────────────────────────────────
  :renders-as
  "The boot record renders to Markdown. :what-this-is / :when-to-read
   / :whats-inside become the page intro; :nav becomes a linked table
   of contents (one list item per chapter, linking to that chapter's
   rendered page, annotated with :purpose). Rosetta owns the render;
   do NOT hand-write a sibling README.md — it is generated from the
   boot record so the two never drift."

  ;; ─── Repetition across books is expected ─────────────────────
  :repetition-note
  "Every book carries its own boot record with the same five prose
   sections. The doctrine each book states will overlap with its
   siblings — that is fine and intended. Different books face
   different audiences (a kid learning to code vs. an SRE rebuilding
   the system vs. an LLM at inference), so each restates what it
   needs in its own voice. Do NOT factor shared prose out into one
   central file: a boot record must stand alone for a cold reader."

  ;; ─── Scope ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
  :applies-to
  ((:book "scheme/reference/"       :kind "reference-manual"    :boot "00-intro.slat")
   (:book "scheme-books/book-of-*"  :kind "learning-book"       :boot "per book (ch01-*.book.slatl)")
   (:book "SRE-MANUAL/"             :kind "operations-manual"   :boot "per manifest")
   (:book "docs/"                   :kind "engineering-shelf"   :boot "the shelf README lists the recovery books"))
  :does-not-apply-to
  "Plain folders with an orientation README but no chapters
   (scratch/, scripts/, tools/, training/). Those keep a simple
   README.slat and are not books."

  ;; ─── The single rule ─────────────────────────────────────────
  :the-rule
  "One book = one folder = one README.slatl boot record + ordered
   chapters (+ a MANIFEST.slat for programs). The boot record names
   the first chapter, lists the rest in order, and briefs the reader
   in prose. Same shape in every book. Do not drift.")

;; End of book-layout spec. New book folders copy scheme/reference/
;; README.slatl as the template and edit the five prose sections +
;; the :nav list.
